x = 1 + 3t \\
y = -1 + kt \\
z = -3 + 7t
\end{cases}
$$
And the second line is:
$$
\begin{cases}
x = 2 + u \\
y = 4 + 4u \\
z = 6 + 7u
\end{cases}
$$

Now, for two lines to be coplanar, the volume of the parallelepiped formed by the vectors connecting a point on one line to a point on the other line and the direction vectors of the two lines must be zero. The volume is calculated using the scalar triple product of these three vectors. If the scalar triple product is zero, the lines are coplanar.

So, let me recall the formula. The scalar triple product is $ (\mathbf{a} \cdot (\mathbf{b} \times \mathbf{c})) $. 

In this case, I need to take a vector connecting a point on the first line to a point on the second line, which is $\mathbf{a} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 - 1 \\ 4 - (-1) \\ 6 - (-3) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 5 \\ 9 \end{pmatrix}$.

